% A cell on the spine is not exposed (tasks.org,
% aida-curried-intermediate-conclusions, decided 2026-10-13; the horn
% rule of aida-discuss-non-site-attack-points): cArg derives C from
% abc : A->(B->C), an obligation for A and a presumption of B.  B->C
% occurs in cArg's body only as the type of the elimination stack's
% tail, a cell carrying cArg's own continuation r, so it is on the spine
% and no node of cArg.  The counterargument test, a bare presumption
% against B->C, is reachable from nothing and takes no part in cArg's
% term.
lk.
declare A,B,C:bool.
declare abc : (A->(B->C)).
declare axA : (A).

argument cArg : (C).
cut (A->(B->C)) r.
axiom abc.
elim.
next.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom r.
dixi.

argument aArg : (A).
axiom axA.
dixi.

counterargument test : (B->C).
by default.
dixi.

evaluate cArg.
